% The only model whose output contains `bool` items, so the only one that pins the
% false/true half of the FlatZinc output format (SPEC 2.2) through the CLI.
%
% A bool reaches the printer by three routes, and this model takes all three:
%
%   `aliased' -- a `var bool' aliased to a bool *parameter*. The builder folds the
%                parameter to a constant while resolving names, so nothing about the
%                value says it was ever a bool. This is the M1-T21 bug: before the fix
%                it printed `aliased = 1;', which is not valid FlatZinc output.
%   `flags'   -- an `output_array' of `var bool' whose elements are folded constants
%                too, one of each truth value.
%   `b'       -- an honest `var bool' that the search has to assign, and which the
%                printer used to get right, because a real variable still carries its
%                declared domain.
%
% `x' is here so that one model shows both print styles: 1 as an integer next to 1 as a
% bool. The solution is unique -- int_eq pins x, b is the only free variable and 0 is
% the only value in its domain that... is not forced at all: b is unconstrained, so the
% default search of SPEC 3.4 (input_order, indomain_min) settles it at false. That is
% the same reliance trivial_sat.fzn has on the default strategy, and if the default ever
% changes this test is supposed to notice.
bool: t = true;
bool: f = false;
var bool: aliased :: output_var = t;
array [1..2] of var bool: flags :: output_array([1..2]) = [f, t];
var bool: b :: output_var;
var 1..3: x :: output_var;
constraint int_eq(x, 2);
solve satisfy;
